The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 2216 (2015), 2722 (2024), 2739 (2024), 2768 (2025), and 2787 (2025), as well as other relevant resolutions,

Reaffirming its commitment to the sovereignty, territorial integrity, and political independence of Yemen and of all States in the region,

Reaffirming international law, as reflected in the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea of 10 December 1982 (UNCLOS), including the rights and freedoms of navigation,

Expressing grave concern over the continuing attacks by the Houthis against commercial and merchant vessels in the Red Sea and surrounding waterways, which disrupt international commerce and jeopardize the safety of maritime routes,

Expressing serious concern over the severe environmental and ecological risks posed by attacks on vessels carrying hazardous materials, petroleum, and other cargo, and emphasizing the necessity of preventing environmental catastrophe in the Red Sea marine environment,

Expressing deep concern regarding the impact of these attacks on the delivery of vital humanitarian assistance to the people of Yemen and to the wider region, as well as on global supply chains and food security,

Reiterating the urgent need to address the root causes of regional tensions, and emphasizing the imperative of diplomatic efforts to de-escalate tensions and promote lasting peace and security in the region,

1. Condemns in the strongest terms the continued Houthi attacks against commercial and merchant vessels in the Red Sea and adjacent waters, and demands that the Houthis immediately cease all such attacks;

2. Demands the immediate and unconditional release of the crew of the MV Galaxy Leader;

3. Urges caution and restraint to prevent further escalation of the situation in the Red Sea and the broader region, and calls on all parties to respect international law, including international humanitarian law;

4. Emphasizes the need to prevent environmental hazards resulting from attacks on commercial vessels, and calls for unhindered access and support for international response efforts to mitigate maritime environmental threats;

5. Urges all parties to facilitate the unhindered flow of humanitarian aid and commercial goods, noting the critical importance of maritime trade routes to the provision of humanitarian relief to the Yemeni population;

6. Extends the reporting request in paragraph 10 of resolution 2722 (2024) through July 15, 2026;

7. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 2216 (2015), 2722 (2024), 2739 (2024), 2768 (2025), and 2787 (2025), as well as other relevant resolutions,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, unity, territorial integrity, and political independence of Yemen and of all States in the region, and stressing the importance of strictly upholding the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Emphasizing that the primary responsibility for ensuring maritime security and stability in the Red Sea rests with its coastal and littoral States, with the support of the international community,

Reaffirming international law, as reflected in the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea of 10 December 1982 (UNCLOS), including the rights and freedoms of navigation,

