Expressing deep concern regarding the impact of these attacks on the delivery of vital humanitarian assistance to the people of Yemen and to the wider region, as well as on global supply chains and food security,

Reiterating the urgent need to address the root causes of regional tensions, particularly the conflict and humanitarian crisis in the Gaza Strip, reiterating calls for an immediate and durable ceasefire in Gaza and the full implementation of relevant Security Council resolutions, and emphasizing the imperative of diplomatic efforts to de-escalate tensions and promote lasting peace and security in the region,

1. Condemns in the strongest terms the continued Houthi attacks against commercial and merchant vessels in the Red Sea and adjacent waters, and demands that the Houthis immediately cease all such attacks;

2. Takes note of the right of Member States, in accordance with international law, to defend their vessels from attacks, including those that undermine navigational rights and freedoms;

3. Demands the immediate and unconditional release of the MV Galaxy Leader and its crew, as well as all other unlawfully detained seafarers, and calls for the safety, protection, and well-being of all merchant mariners and seafarers operating in the region;

4. Calls upon all Member States to adhere strictly to their obligations under the targeted arms embargo pursuant to resolution 2216 (2015) and subsequent resolutions, urges continued cooperation to prevent the Houthis from acquiring weapons and the materiel necessary to carry out further attacks, and condemns violations of the arms embargo;

5. Urges caution and restraint to prevent further escalation of the situation in the Red Sea and the broader region, calls on all parties to respect international law, including the Charter of the United Nations and international humanitarian law, and emphasizes that actions taken to protect maritime navigation must strictly conform to international law and respect the sovereignty and territorial integrity of Yemen and of all coastal States;

6. Reaffirms that nothing in this resolution, or in resolution 2722 (2024) and subsequent resolutions, shall be interpreted as authorizing or providing a legal basis for the use of armed force against the territory of the sovereign State of Yemen;

7. Emphasizes the need to prevent environmental hazards resulting from attacks on commercial vessels, and calls for unhindered access and support for international response efforts to mitigate maritime environmental threats;

8. Urges all parties to facilitate the unhindered flow of humanitarian aid and commercial goods, noting the critical importance of maritime trade routes to the provision of humanitarian relief to the Yemeni population;

9. Extends the reporting request in paragraph 10 of resolution 2722 (2024) through July 15, 2026;

10. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

